[FINAL-TEXT] All evidence is now complete. The packet counter confirms it unambiguously: rule 4 (ICMP DROP on `in:eth2`) has exactly 3 packets / 252 bytes — precisely matching pc15's 3-ping test. Routers router0, router1, router2, router3 all carry kernel static routes back to 172.18.50/24 and 172.18.51/24, so the routing layer is not broken — the iptables firewall on router6 is the sole cause.

=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
The fault lies in router6's iptables FORWARD chain, which has three recently-added DROP rules that discard all TCP, UDP, and ICMP traffic arriving on eth2 — the upstream interface connecting router6 to router3 and the rest of the network. Only a single narrow exception (rule 1) permits TCP port 80 from 172.16.10.0/24 destined to 172.18.51.1.

The mechanism is straightforward: Location B hosts (PC15-18, Laptop4/5) can send outbound packets without issue — those packets exit router6 via eth2 in the forward direction (matching `in:eth1` or `in:eth0`, which carry no DROP rules). However, every reply packet from Location A or Location C re-enters router6 on eth2, immediately hitting rules 2-4 and being silently dropped. Without stateful tracking (no `ESTABLISHED/RELATED` permit), there is no way for any response to get through. Direct proof: the ICMP DROP rule (rule 4) shows exactly 3 packets / 252 bytes — matching pc15's three 84-byte test pings to a destination that router6 and router3 can otherwise reach.

Routing itself is intact: router0, router1, router2, and router3 all carry kernel static routes for 172.18.50.0/24 and 172.18.51.0/24 pointing back toward router6, and router6 has valid RIP-learned routes to all remote subnets. The only break in the data plane is this firewall policy on router6.

Root cause: Broad blanket DROP rules on `in:eth2` were added to router6's FORWARD chain, blocking all return traffic destined for Location B.

Corrective fix: Remove rules 2, 3, and 4 from the FORWARD chain (`iptables -D FORWARD` for each). If stateful protection is required, insert an `iptables -I FORWARD 1 -m state --state ESTABLISHED,RELATED -j ACCEPT` rule before the DROPs to allow return traffic for Location B-initiated sessions.
=== END ===